---
import Marketing from '~/layouts/Marketing.astro';
import Velocity from '~/components/Velocity.astro';
import { SITE, FACTS, REPO_URL, blob, fmtDate } from '~/lib/site';
const n = SITE.name;
---

<Marketing title={`Why ${n}`} description={`Why ${n} exists, what it has measured, and where it's going.`}>
  <header class="m-pagehead m-narrow">
    <p class="m-eyebrow">Why {n}</p>
    <h1>I wanted Claude Code to remember, and to keep working when I walk away.</h1>
    <p>
      So I built that around it. This page is the case for it: the problem, how {n} goes at it, what I've measured, and
      where it's heading. Every number links to how it was counted.
    </p>
  </header>

  <article class="m-narrow m-prose">
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      Claude Code is a great pair programmer with no memory. Every session starts cold. You re-explain the project, the
      decision you made last week, the mistake you already fixed. Then the session ends and it's gone again.
    </p>
    <p>
      Letting an agent run on its own is the other half. Left alone, agents ship junk: changes nobody measured, tests
      that pass because they test nothing, PRs that merge on top of a broken main. Speed without checks just makes more
      to clean up.
    </p>

    <h2>The approach</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Choose the context.</strong> Memory, decisions and a shared vocabulary live outside the model. A session
        loads the ones the task needs, and skills load only when they're called. <a href="/docs/features/memory/">Memory</a>,
        <a href="/docs/features/skills/">skills</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Gate every change.</strong> The pipeline works in its own worktree, measures its result, and keeps it only
        if nothing got worse. Nothing reaches main without my Approve and a gate that re-checks it.
        <a href="/docs/features/ticket-pipeline/">Pipeline</a>, <a href="/docs/features/merge-gate/">merge gate</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Measure every claim.</strong> If {n} says something helps, there's a bench run or a count behind it, with the
        method in the repo. When a measurement says I was wrong, the default changes.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What I've measured</h2>
    <p>
      From <a href={blob('bench/SCORECARD.md')}>the bench</a>: the same questions asked of plain Claude Code (clean), a
      trimmed setup (lean) and {n}. Scorecard last updated 5 Sep 2026. These are small runs, so read them as direction,
      not proof at scale.
    </p>

    <h3>It knows things plain Claude Code can't</h3>
    <p>
      Asked what goes wrong when {n}'s scripts run outside their own Python environment, only {n} answered correctly.
      The answer lives in its memory, not in any file. The cost difference was near zero (18,418 vs 18,998 tokens).
    </p>

    <h3>It finds things with half the tokens</h3>
    <div class="m-table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Setup</th><th>Tokens</th><th>Tool calls</th><th>Correct</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Plain Claude Code</td><td>95,682</td><td>6</td><td>yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Lean</td><td>66,762</td><td>4</td><td>yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>{n}</td><td><strong>45,905</strong></td><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>yes</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>All three got there. {n} used 52% fewer tokens and a third of the tool calls, because it knew where to look.</p>

    <h3>Recall doesn't need the expensive model</h3>
    <div class="m-table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Cost per recall task</th><th>Quality</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>A local 14B model with {n}'s retrieval</td><td>$0.00</td><td>Right meaning, judged</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Claude Haiku with {n}</td><td>about $0.02</td><td>Exact phrase</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Claude Sonnet with {n}</td><td>about $0.05</td><td>Both</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>I measured my own tools, and dropped one</h3>
    <p>
      I added a code knowledge graph and told every session to search it first. Then I tested it: 30 real questions with
      known answers, scored without an AI judge. Plain keyword search put the answer in the top five 67% of the time.
      The graph managed 38%. On questions that need two files, keyword search found 3 of 4 and the graph found none. So
      the rule flipped to keyword search first, and the graph kept the structure questions it's good at.
      <a href={blob('bench/RESULTS.md')}>Results, 26 Oct 2026</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>It ships</h2>
    <Velocity />
    <p>
      Green is code the pipeline wrote on its own, then I reviewed and merged. Amber is work done in a session with
      me. Most of the pipeline's count landed in one week in early October (64 PRs). Since then most merges have come
      from sessions with me, a lot of it tightening the gates the pipeline has to pass.
    </p>
    <p>
      The proof that counts most is outside this repo. <a href={SITE.builtWith[0].url}>{SITE.builtWith[0].name}</a> is a
      live-captioning app for iPhone that I built with {n}. It went from first commit to the App Store in 50 days, and
      19 of its merged PRs came from the pipeline.
    </p>

    <h2>Open engine, optional add-ons</h2>
    <p>
      The engine is open source and stays free: memory, skills, the pipeline, the gates, the dashboard. Each person's own
      state lives in their own private repo, never in the engine. Later, optional add-ons will sit on top for people who
      want more, each in its own repo. The rule I hold myself to: the free engine has to be good on its own. An add-on
      earns its place by doing something new, not by taking something away.
    </p>

    <h2>Where it's going</h2>
    <p>These are the north stars every change gets checked against, in plain words:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Do more with less.</strong> A change only counts if quality holds at the same or lower cost in tokens, compute and time.</li>
      <li><strong>Be reachable from anywhere.</strong> Phone, laptop, any machine, working offline, and never breaking silently.</li>
      <li><strong>Leave people more capable, not more passive.</strong> If doing a thing yourself makes you better at it, {n} should coach, not take over.</li>
      <li><strong>Be the best tool for making tools.</strong> Work that makes {n} better at building and shipping things beats work that builds one thing.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      What's being built now and next is on the <a href="/roadmap/">roadmap</a>. What changed recently is in the
      <a href="/changelog/">changelog</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="how-we-counted">How each number was counted</h2>
    <p class="m-dim">All counted on {fmtDate(FACTS.counted_on)}.</p>
    <div class="m-table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Number</th><th>What it counts</th><th>How</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            FACTS.items.map((f) => (
              <tr>
                <td><strong>{f.value}</strong> {f.label}</td>
                <td>{f.detail}</td>
                <td><code>{f.command}</code></td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Who builds it</h2>
    <p>
      I'm {SITE.builder}. I build {n} for my own work first and use it every day. More on the <a href="/about/">about page</a>.
      The code is on <a href={REPO_URL}>GitHub</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Marketing>

<style>
  .m-prose code {
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    word-break: break-word;
  }
</style>
